---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `SUP Valve Types — Halkey Roberts, Military, and Beyond | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Complete guide to inflatable SUP valve types: Halkey-Roberts, military-style, Boston, and leaf valves. How they work, compatibility, maintenance, and replacement.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'SUP Valve Types Guide',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Valve Types</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Engineering</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">6 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">SUP Valve Types Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      The valve is the most mechanically complex component on an inflatable SUP. Understanding valve types,
      compatibility, and maintenance prevents inflation problems and extends board life.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>Halkey-Roberts Valve</h2>
      <p>
        The most common SUP valve standard. Halkey-Roberts (HR) valves feature a spring-loaded central pin that opens
        when pressed, allowing rapid deflation. The valve body is threaded to accept a screw-on pump adapter. HR valves
        are designed for high-pressure inflation (12-20 PSI) and are the preferred standard across the SUP industry.
        Advantages include wide pump compatibility, easy replacement (threaded base), and reliable sealing at high
        pressure. Some budget HR valves use plastic internals that can crack — quality valves use brass or stainless
        steel internals.
      </p>
      <h2>Military-Style Valve</h2>
      <p>
        Also called "military" or "leaf" valves, these are simpler than HR valves with fewer moving parts. They use a
        spring-loaded flap rather than a central pin. Deflation requires manually holding the flap open. Military valves
        are common on lower-cost SUPs and some European brands. They are less prone to mechanical failure (fewer parts)
        but harder to deflate quickly and less compatible with standard pump adapters. Some paddlers prefer them for
        river running where simplicity and robustness outweigh convenience.
      </p>
      <h2>Boston Valve</h2>
      <p>
        A two-part valve system with an inner plug and outer cap. Boston valves are commonly found on kayaks and
        whitewater SUPs. The inner plug seals the air chamber; the outer cap protects the plug. They allow high-volume
        airflow for fast inflation/deflation but require two hands to operate. Boston valves are less common on
        recreational SUPs but are standard on many whitewater-specific boards where rapid deflation for self-rescue is
        critical.
      </p>
      <h2>Valve Maintenance</h2>
      <p>
        All valve types require periodic inspection. Check the O-ring or gasket for cracks or deformation — a damaged
        gasket causes slow leaks. Clean valve threads with a soft brush to remove sand and debris. Apply silicone-based
        valve lubricant (not petroleum-based, which degrades rubber) annually. For HR valves, test the spring action —
        the pin should snap back firmly. If the valve leaks around the base (not through the opening), the base gasket
        may need replacement or tightening.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
